# The Supabase project behind the waitlist, sign-in, and the account page.
#
# Both of these are browser-safe by design. The publishable key identifies the
# project and grants nothing on its own: row level security decides what any
# request may actually read, and it is already public, because every visitor to
# this site downloads it. The service role key, which does bypass row level
# security, belongs only in the gateway's environment and never here.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# The Metis AI gateway. Only the account page calls it, and only to manage
# bring-your-own-provider connections — everything else the site needs it asks
# Supabase for directly. Leave it blank and that one section says so rather than
# failing.
VITE_METIS_API_URL=https://metis-api-staging.onrender.com

# Optional. Crash reporting for this website, and nothing else.
#
# Leave it out entirely and there is no error reporting: no Sentry request, no
# console warning, and not even the SDK, which is behind a dynamic import that
# is never reached. That is the state every build is in today, because there is
# no Sentry account yet. Setting it is how you turn the feature on, and doing so
# means the privacy policy's list of who touches user data becomes true of you.
#
# A DSN is public — it is compiled into the browser bundle and identifies a
# project to send events to. It is not a credential and grants nothing.
#
# Before switching it on, read the scrubbing in src/lib/telemetry.ts. Access
# tokens, provider keys and email addresses are stripped from every event, and
# that promise is made out loud on the privacy page.
# VITE_SENTRY_DSN=https://examplePublicKey@o0.ingest.sentry.io/0
